INT. AIRPLANE HANGAR - ROLLER DERBY TRACK - NIGHT

A banked oval of painted concrete. Folding chairs. A rusting 747 fuselage looms behind the bleachers.

Four skaters huddle at the bench, sweating through their jerseys. BEX LUNDQVIST (30s, jammer, star on her helmet). TAMSIN FALL (20s, blocker, bruised shin). KALANI AKANA (30s, captain, scarred knuckles). COACH RUIZ (50s, gravel voice, whistle, a stopwatch) paces in front of them.

COACH RUIZ
Eight seconds, Bex. Eight seconds you stood in the pack and counted your own feet.

BEX
I was reading the wall.

COACH RUIZ
The wall isn't moving.

BEX
Neither was I.

KALANI
(to the team)
Listen up. Down by nine. Two minutes. Tam, you take the inside. Bex, you go on my whistle.

TAMSIN
Whose whistle?

KALANI
Mine.

TAMSIN
You don't have a whistle.

COACH RUIZ
(muttering)
Dear God.

KALANI
I will make a noise like a whistle.

BEX
Can you make it loud?

KALANI
I can make it louder than the coach.

COACH RUIZ
That's not hard.

TAMSIN
(quiet, to herself)
I'm gonna throw up. I'm gonna throw up on the 747.

KALANI
Tamsin. Look at me.

TAMSIN
I'm looking.

KALANI
You're going to hit the wall at forty miles an hour, and you're going to get back up, and you're going to do it again until they stop counting.

TAMSIN
And if I don't?

KALANI
Then I'll hit it for you.

Coach Ruiz clicks the stopwatch. The crowd, small and loud, begins to stomp.

COACH RUIZ
Go. Go, go, go.

They skate out in a rolling blur, wheels thunder on the concrete, and the 747 watches from the dark.
